Spec: Plugin Installer Service

Purpose:
  Install or refresh a named plugin from Git and maintain its source, requested version, and content checksum in the current SpecDD project.

Owns:
  ./plugin-installer.ts
  ./plugin-installer.test.ts

References:
  ../plugin-source/plugin-source.sdd
  ../plugin-registry/plugin-registry.sdd
  ../logger/logger.sdd
  ../plugin-signature-verifier/plugin-signature-verifier.sdd
  /src/infrastructure/confirmation-prompt.sdd
  /src/infrastructure/git-client.sdd
  /src/infrastructure/file-system.sdd
  /src/cli-error.sdd

Must:
  Accept @PluginSource, @PluginRegistry, @GitClient, @FileSystem, @Logger, @PluginSignatureVerifier, and @ConfirmationPrompt through constructor parameters.
  Accept an absolute project directory, repository input, plugin name, and optional version.
  Require `.specdd/bootstrap.md` in the supplied project directory before downloading or writing project files.
  Resolve the source through @PluginSource and validate the existing registry before downloading.
  @PluginInstaller.add installs only the selected plugin file at `.specdd/plugins/<repocoords>/<pluginname>/plugin.md`.
  @PluginInstaller.refresh requires the registration's resolved `src` to match its managed installation path and retains its original source spelling.
  Before refreshing, verify under the project lock that the registration still exists and its `src`, `origin`, `sig`, and `version` match the selected registration; reject a concurrent registration change before downloading or writing.
  Require the registry, lock, managed destination, and their existing ancestor paths inside the project to be free of symlinks that redirect project writes.
  Reject a managed destination occupied by a non-regular file or an existing file without a matching registry entry.
  Before replacing a registered file, verify that its bytes match the recorded `sig`; fail without overwriting locally modified content.
  Restore a missing registered file from the requested source when its destination is otherwise safe.
  Read the source file through @GitClient and compute its SHA-256 over the returned bytes without text decoding or newline conversion.
  Read the plugin and its optional detached signature at the same commit, and verify through @PluginSignatureVerifier for both add and refresh before managed writes or a no-op result.
  Require `vendorOnly` verification for official repositories identified by @PluginSource; only a verified vendor result permits add, refresh, or a no-op result.
  Official-source verification failures raise @PluginInstallError naming the source and the embedded-key requirement, without prompting or changing managed plugin files or registry contents, and release the project lock.
  For other repositories, when verification does not establish an accepted signer, show a reason-specific warning through @Logger even when ordinary warnings are filtered, and require explicit confirmation through @ConfirmationPrompt.
  Include the source identity and resolved commit with warnings; distinguish missing or invalid signatures from valid signatures with insufficient identity validity, showing the latter's full fingerprint and validity.
  Refusal raises @PluginInstallError without changing managed files or registry contents, and releases the project lock.
  Recheck managed destination safety, plugin bytes, and the target registration's `src`, `origin`, `sig`, and `version` after verification and confirmation, before replacement or a no-op result; reject registrations that appear, disappear, or change while the operation is pending.
  Record `origin` as the normalized origin repository URL followed by `#` and the full resolved commit identifier.
  Record `src` as the managed relative destination for new installations, preserve it for refreshes, and record `version` as supplied by @PluginSource.
  Protect registry read, destination checks, plugin replacement, and registry upsert with an exclusive project lock at `.specdd/plugins.lock`.
  Report lock contention without mutating the registry or managed files.
  Stage downloaded bytes before replacing the managed file, then commit the registry update through @PluginRegistry with the preceding registration or expected absence as a precondition.
  On registry update failure, restore the previous managed file or remove a newly installed file; report any rollback failure with the affected path.
  Return a no-op only when the installed bytes and all generated registry fields already match, including `version`.
  Preserve unrelated registrations and managed plugin files when installing or replacing one plugin.
  Report the resolved commit, requested version, installed path, registry path, and installed, replaced, or unchanged outcome through @Logger and the result.

Must not:
  Write plugin contents to external `src` paths obtained from existing registry entries.
  Execute downloaded plugin contents or register other files from the source repository.

Depends on:
  @PluginSource
  @PluginRegistry
  @GitClient
  @FileSystem
  @Logger
  Node.js SHA-256 hashing

Exposes:
  @PluginInstaller
  @PluginInstaller.add
  @PluginInstaller.refresh

Accepts:
  @PluginAddRequest
  @PluginRefreshRequest

Returns:
  @PluginAddResult

Raises:
  @PluginInstallError extending @CliError for project state, destination conflicts, lock contention, and write or rollback failures

Scenario: omitted version
  Given an initialized project with no plugin registry
  When `review` is added from `\@acme/specdd-plugins` without a version
  Then `.plugins/review/plugin.md` from the remote default branch is installed at `.specdd/plugins/github.com/acme/specdd-plugins/review/plugin.md`
  And the registry records `version` equal to `latest`, the resolved commit in `origin`, and the installed bytes' checksum in `sig`

Scenario: explicit commit version
  Given a commit identifier reachable from a repository branch or tag was supplied as the version
  When a plugin is installed from that commit
  Then `version` preserves the supplied commit identifier verbatim
  And `origin` records its full resolved commit identifier

Scenario: same content with a different requested version
  Given a plugin was installed without a version
  And tag `v1.2.0` resolves to the already installed commit and bytes
  When that plugin is added with version `v1.2.0`
  Then its existing registry entry records `version` equal to `v1.2.0`

Scenario: unchanged installation
  Given the installed bytes and registry fields match the requested installation
  When the same plugin is added again
  Then the result is unchanged
  And the plugin file and registry are not rewritten

Scenario: locally modified plugin
  Given an installed plugin file no longer matches its recorded checksum
  When the same plugin is added again
  Then a @PluginInstallError is raised
  And the plugin file and registry are unchanged

Scenario: registry write failure
  Given a registered plugin file matches its recorded checksum
  When replacement bytes are installed but registry replacement fails
  Then the preceding plugin bytes are restored and the preceding registry remains intact
  And the installation reports failure

Scenario: registration edited while verification is pending
  Given a registered plugin is being refreshed
  And its registration is removed or its source, origin, checksum, or version changes during verification or confirmation
  When verification or confirmation completes
  Then installation fails without overwriting the registry edit or changing the plugin bytes
  And the project lock is released

Scenario: registration edited during plugin replacement
  Given the selected registration matched before the plugin file was replaced
  And its registration changes before the registry update
  When the registry precondition rejects the update
  Then the previous plugin bytes are restored and the registry edit is preserved

Scenario: missing source file
  Given the selected commit has no `.plugins/review/plugin.md`
  When `review` is added
  Then installation fails without changing managed plugin files or registry contents

Scenario: concurrent installation
  Given another installation holds the project's plugin lock
  When a plugin is added
  Then a @PluginInstallError reports lock contention
  And that attempt does not change managed plugin files or registry contents

Scenario: unsigned plugin refused
  Given the source has no detached signature
  When the operator declines the default-No confirmation
  Then installation fails without creating a plugin file or registry
  And the project lock is released

Scenario: untrusted identity
  Given a valid signature has marginal identity validity in the local keyring
  When the plugin is added or refreshed
  Then the warning states that the signature is valid but identity trust is insufficient
  And only explicit confirmation permits installation

Scenario: official plugin without a vendor signature
  Given a plugin comes from a repository under `github.com/specdd/`
  And its signature is absent, invalid, expired, revoked, or made by a key outside the bundled SpecDD keys
  When the plugin is added or refreshed
  Then installation fails with an error explaining that an embedded-key signature is required
  And no confirmation prompt is offered and the plugin and registry remain unchanged
